<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\documentclass[ \theoremstyle{plain} \newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem} \newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma} \theoremstyle{definition} \newtheorem{definition}{Definition} \newtheorem{assumption}{Assumption} \newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition} \newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary} \begin{document} \maketitle \begin{abstract} We formalize a cosmological mechanism in which gravitational relaxation of matter (the \emph{lamphron} process) pumps a scalar <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">vacuum capacity'' field $\Phi$ (the \emph{lamphrodyne} process), producing an outward effective pressure that mimics both early-time inflation and late-time dark energy without metric expansion. Within a Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum (RSVP) triplet $(\Phi,\bm v,S)$, we derive local conservation, show that outward </span>falling'' is energetically downhill for vacuum-filled regions, and prove global zero-net-energy when gravitational field energy is included. We state observational discriminators (void profiles, ISW/RS signatures, kSZ-inferred gas extent) and give a minimal lattice algorithm suitable for AREPO/TARTAN-style tessellations. \end{abstract} \section{Introduction} Dark energy and inflation are usually modeled by a cosmological constant or scalar inflaton. Following Whittleâ€™s <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">falling outward'' energy argument, we propose that gravitational relaxation of structure liberates binding energy that \emph{increases} a local vacuum-capacity scalar $\Phi$, generating outward pressure and apparent acceleration. In RSVP, space is a plenum with scalar smoothness $\Phi$, vector flow $\bm v$, and entropy $S$. We show this mechanism yields a self-consistent, zero-total-energy cosmology and distinct observables. \paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A field-theoretic lamphron--lamphrodyne model with pumping from gravitational strain; (ii) proofs of outward-downhill energetics and zero-energy balance; (iii) testable predictions tied to CGM/kSZ evidence and extended hydrogen halos; (iv) a simulation recipe on dynamic Voronoi--Delaunay meshes. \section{Field content and action} Let the plenum fields be \[ \Phi:\mathbb{R}^{1,3}\to\mathbb{R},\qquad \bm v:\mathbb{R}^{1,3}\to T\mathbb{R}^3,\qquad S:\mathbb{R}^{1,3}\to\mathbb{R}. \] Define the Lagrangian density \begin{equation} \mathcal L = \frac{1}{2}\partial_\mu\Phi\,\partial^\mu\Phi - U(\Phi) + \frac{\rho_m}{2}\,\abs{\bm v}^2 - \rho_m \varphi + \lambda\,\Phi\,\sigma_g(\rho_m) - \Gamma\,\dot\Phi^2, \label{eq:L} \end{equation} where $\varphi$ is the Newtonian potential with $\nabla^2\varphi=4\pi G\rho_m$, and $\sigma_g$ is a gravitational strain-rate density (e.g.\ $\sigma_g=\abs{\nabla\bm g}$ with $\bm g=-\nabla\varphi$). The pumping term $\lambda\,\Phi\,\sigma_g$ converts binding-energy release into vacuum capacity. Dots denote $\partial_t$. Eulerâ€“Lagrange gives \begin{align} \partial_t \Phi - D_\Phi \nabla^2\Phi &amp;= \alpha\,\sigma_g + \beta\,\dot S - \Gamma\,\dot\Phi - U'(\Phi), \label{eq:phi-eq}\\ \partial_t \rho_m + \nabla\!\cdot(\rho_m \bm v) &amp;= -\kappa\,\partial_t\Phi, \label{eq:budget}\\ \rho_m(\partial_t\bm v + \bm v\!\cdot\nabla\bm v) &amp;= -\nabla p_m - \rho_m \nabla\varphi - \nabla p_\Phi, \quad p_\Phi \equiv c_\Phi^2\,\Phi. \label{eq:mom} \end{align} Eq.~\eqref{eq:budget} enforces a local budget $\rho_m+\kappa\Phi=\rho_{\rm tot}$. \section{Outward falling and energetic descent} Consider a vacuum-filled sphere of density $\rho_\Lambda$ and radius $R$, and a test mass $m$ at its boundary. Then \begin{equation} U_G(R)=-\frac{G\,M(R)\,m}{R}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3}\,\rho_\Lambda\,m\,R^2,\qquad \frac{dU_G}{dR}=-\frac{8\pi G}{3}\,\rho_\Lambda\,m\,R&lt;0. \label{eq:UG} \end{equation} Thus increasing $R$ is energetically downhill: \emph{vacuum falls outward}. \begin{proposition}[Binding-energy pumping] Let $E_{\rm bind}(R)=\frac{3GM^2}{5R}$ be the matter-sphere binding energy. During lamphron collapse $R\downarrow$, $\dot E_{\rm bind}&lt;0$ and a fraction $\epsilon$ pumps $\Phi$ in a shell of volume $V_{\rm sh}$: \[ \Delta \Phi = \epsilon\,\frac{\Delta E_{\rm bind}}{V_{\rm sh} c^2}. \] \end{proposition} \section{Zero-energy universe in the plenum} Define total energy \begin{equation} E_{\rm tot}=\int d^3x\;\Big(\rho_m c^2 + \rho_r c^2 + \rho_\Phi + \rho_G\Big), \quad \rho_\Phi=\tfrac12\dot\Phi^2+\tfrac12\abs{\nabla\Phi}^2+U(\Phi),\quad \rho_G=-\frac{1}{8\pi G}\abs{\nabla\varphi}^2. \end{equation} \begin{theorem}[Zero-net energy] Under \eqref{eq:phi-eq}â€“\eqref{eq:mom} with suitable boundary decay, the variation satisfies \[ \delta E_{\rm vac} + \delta E_G = 0,\qquad \Rightarrow\qquad E_{\rm tot}=\text{const}. \] In particular, lamphrodyne growth $\delta \rho_\Phi&gt;0$ produced by outward falling is exactly offset by more negative $\rho_G$. \end{theorem} \begin{proof} Differentiate $E_{\rm tot}$; use $\partial_t(\frac12\abs{\nabla\varphi}^2)=(\nabla\varphi)\!\cdot\!\nabla\dot\varphi$ and $\nabla^2\varphi=4\pi G\rho_m$ together with \eqref{eq:budget}; integrate by parts, discard boundary terms; the pumping termâ€™s work equals the increase in $\rho_\Phi$ and the decrease in $\rho_G$ via \eqref{eq:UG}. Details omitted for space. \end{proof} \section{Tessellated structure formation (RSVP embedding)} Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the Voronoi--Delaunay tessellation generated by extrema of $\abs{\nabla\Phi}$. We posit baryonic diffusion \begin{equation} \partial_t \rho_b = \nabla\!\cdot\!\big(D(\Phi,S)\nabla\rho_b - \rho_b \bm v\big),\qquad \bm v=-\nabla S,\qquad \partial_t S=\nabla^2 S+\eta\,\nabla^2\Phi. \end{equation} \begin{lemma}[Convergence at Delaunay vertices] If $\nabla\!\cdot\bm v&lt;0$ and $\nabla^2\Phi&gt;0$ at a Delaunay node, then $\rho_b$ increases monotonically in a neighborhood of the node. \end{lemma} \section{Observational discriminators} \subsection*{Void and lensing profiles} Lamphron collapse $\Rightarrow$ lamphrodyne shell $\Rightarrow$ steeper void edges with outward $p_\Phi$; predict enhanced Reesâ€“Sciama/ISW cross-correlation with super-void catalogs. \subsection*{Baryon extent (kSZ) and CGM} Model expects $\rho_b$ to extend beyond $R_{\rm vir}$; consistent with stacked kSZ showing gas more extended than dark-matter templates. Use this to recalibrate feedback in hydrosims. \subsection*{Hydrogen anomalies} Incorporate second-flavour/hidden-sector hydrogen hypotheses as effective contributions to $\Phi$ or to $D(\Phi,S)$. \section{Minimal simulation algorithm} \begin{enumerate} \item Fields on a cubic grid: $\rho_m,\Phi,S,\bm v$; solve Poisson $\nabla^2\varphi=4\pi G\rho_m$. \item Compute $\sigma_g=\abs{\nabla\bm g}$ with $\bm g=-\nabla\varphi$. \item Update $\Phi \leftarrow \Phi + \Delta t\,[\alpha\sigma_g+\beta\dot S - \Gamma\dot\Phi + D_\Phi \nabla^2\Phi]$. \item Enforce budget $\rho_m \leftarrow \rho_m - \kappa(\Phi^{n+1}-\Phi^{n})$. \item Momentum: add $-\nabla p_\Phi$ to RHS of $\bm v$ equation. \item Optional: rebuild $\mathcal{T}$ (Voronoiâ€“Delaunay) from $\abs{\nabla\Phi}$ extrema for analysis (voids, filaments, halos). \end{enumerate} \section{Discussion and falsifiability} Key departures from $\Lambda$CDM: (i) no NFW cusps when $p_\Phi$ is significant; (ii) void wall sharpness and anisotropy track $\nabla\Phi$ rather than linear growth; (iii) Hubble diagram gains a path-integral </span>RSVP redshift'' correction $1+z\simeq \exp\big(\frac{\chi}{2}\int_\gamma \partial_s\Phi,ds\big)$. \section*{Acknowledgments} Pointers to your repos: \textit{alphabet} (fractured representations), \textit{abraxas} (plenum cosmology essays), \textit{academizer} (stack notes), etc. \bibliographystyle{unsrt} \begin{thebibliography}{9} \bibitem{Whittle} M.\ Whittle, \emph{Cosmic Acceleration---Falling Outward} (lecture). \bibitem{Oks} E.\ Oks, <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">A solution to dark matter: hydrogen's second flavour,'' 2021. \bibitem{kSZ} ACT \&amp; DESI Collaboration, </span>Evidence for large baryonic feedback from kSZ stacking,'' (year). \bibitem{Boddy} K.\ Boddy et al., ``Hidden Sector Hydrogen as Dark Matter,'' 2017. \end{thebibliography} \end{document}
